 ### Weight Shifting for Isosurfaces
-A technique to render arbitrary isosurfaces of a Bivariate Tetrahedral (BT) volume by transforming the BT into a space where one weight corresponds to a constant term, then shifting that weight to align the desired isosurface level with the zero-isosurface [S13]. The transformation is $\hat{B}_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3} = T^{-1}_{\beta_1}^{\alpha_1} T^{-1}_{\beta_2}^{\alpha_2} T^{-1}_{\beta_3}^{\alpha_3} B_{\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \alpha_3}$, where $T$ is the bounding tetrahedron matrix [S13]. The modified weight is $B'_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3} = \hat{B}_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3} + d$ if $(\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3) = (3,3,3)$, else $\hat{B}_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3}$, shifting the isosurface by density $d$ [S13]. The weight at position (3,3,3) in the transformed tensor $\hat{B}$ is the only one containing the constant term from the original BT, which corresponds to the homogeneous coordinate $u=1$ in Euclidean space [S13]. Choose this for rendering arbitrary isosurfaces in BT volumes without modifying the underlying data structure.
+A technique to render arbitrary isosurfaces of a Bivariate Tetrahedral (BT) volume by transforming the BT into a space where one weight corresponds to a constant term, then shifting that weight to align the desired isosurface level with the zero-isosurface [S14]. The transformation is $\hat{B}_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3} = T^{-1}_{\beta_1}^{\alpha_1} T^{-1}_{\beta_2}^{\alpha_2} T^{-1}_{\beta_3}^{\alpha_3} B_{\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \alpha_3}$, where $T$ is the bounding tetrahedron matrix [S14]. The modified weight is $B'_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3} = \hat{B}_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3} + d$ if $(\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3) = (3,3,3)$, else $\hat{B}_{\beta_1, \beta_2, \beta_3}$, shifting the isosurface by density $d$ [S14]. The weight at position (3,3,3) in the transformed tensor $\hat{B}$ is the only one containing the constant term from the original BT, which corresponds to the homogeneous coordinate $u=1$ in Euclidean space [S14]. Choose this for rendering arbitrary isosurfaces in BT volumes without modifying the underlying data structure [S14].
 
 ### Opaque Isosurface Rendering with FBM Noise
-Rendering opaque isosurfaces using Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) fields of various noise functions (Perlin, Cos, Worley, Turbulent) with Signed Distance Field (SDF) operators [S14]. It uses FBM of Perlin noise (2nd order Lipschitz) with improved quadratic bounds and enhancements for basic scenes [S14]. For non-2nd-order-Lipschitz noise (e.g., Perlin with abs, Worley, Turbulent), it uses only enhancements applicable to linear bounds [S14]. For Hypertextures, it uses FBM field of Perlin noise with quadratic bounds and varying SDF thresholds, leveraging BVH and Early Stop to avoid manual bounding spheres [S14]. For height fields, it uses FBM with many octaves (e.g., 1000) in shaders, allowing free animation [S14]. Performance speedups include ×4.2 for Sponge scene, ×5.7 for Clouds scene, ×10.6 for Animated Hypertextured sphere, ×4.0 for Animated Amplified wall, ×12.2 for Low $\beta$ (0.4) FBM lacunarity scene, and ×4.7 for High $\beta$ FBM lacunarity scene [S14]. Choose this for procedural noise-based isosurfaces with high performance.
