 ### Pre-computed Color/Opacity Volume Rendering
-A modification of image order algorithms where a color and opacity volume are pre-computed from the original dataset to avoid per-ray shading/classification, at the cost of requiring recomputation when parameters change [S15]. Colors are assigned by using the local gradient as surface normal approximation and performing a local shading calculation [S15]. Opacity values are assigned based on the data and gradient values using a user definable transfer function [S15]. Rays are cast into the two new datasets (color and classified opacity volume) for image generation [S15]. A pitfall is that any change in classification or shading parameters requires the opacity and color arrays to be recomputed, which is prohibitive for higher resolution datasets due to storing gradients and gradient magnitudes [S15]. Choose this for static scenes where shading parameters do not change frequently.
+A modification of image order algorithms where a color and opacity volume are pre-computed from the original dataset to avoid per-ray shading/classification, at the cost of requiring recomputation when parameters change [S16]. Colors are assigned by using the local gradient as surface normal approximation and performing a local shading calculation [S16]. Opacity values are assigned based on the data and gradient values using a user definable transfer function [S16]. Rays are cast into the two new datasets (color and classified opacity volume) for image generation [S16]. A pitfall is that any change in classification or shading parameters requires the opacity and color arrays to be recomputed, which is prohibitive for higher resolution datasets due to storing gradients and gradient magnitudes [S16]. Choose this for static scenes where shading parameters do not change frequently [S16].
 
 ### Iso-Surface Ray-Casting
-A variant of GPU-based ray-casting to render illuminated surfaces, where traversal is back-to-front within each pass to find the closest intersection point, followed by gradient lookup and lighting [S16, unofficial]. In passes 3 to N, it performs M steps back-to-front, fetching scalar value from 3D texture. If value >= threshold, it stores current position $r(t)$ in a temporary variable, keeping only the closest intersection [S16, unofficial]. At the end of the shader, it checks the temporary variable; if not initial value, it looks up 3D gradient texture and performs surface lighting, rendering final color with illumination and opacity 1 [S16, unofficial]. A pitfall is that fragment shaders do not support conditional execution, so gradient reconstruction and illumination are always performed even if no surface is hit, though only once every M steps [S16, unofficial]. Performance: RC-$\beta$ (with empty-space skipping) for Engine dataset (256^2 * 128) achieved 13.6 fps, compared to 6.3 fps for RC and 10.4 fps for SBVR [S16, unofficial]. Choose this for GPU-based isosurface rendering with empty-space skipping.
+A variant of GPU-based ray-casting to render illuminated surfaces, where traversal is back-to-front within each pass to find the closest intersection point, followed by gradient lookup and lighting [S17, unofficial]. In passes 3 to N, it performs M steps back-to-front, fetching scalar value from 3D texture [S17, unofficial]. If value >= threshold, it stores current position $r(t)$ in a temporary variable, keeping only the closest intersection [S17, unofficial]. At the end of the shader, it checks the temporary variable; if not initial value, it looks up 3D gradient texture and performs surface lighting, rendering final color with illumination and opacity 1 [S17, unofficial]. A pitfall is that fragment shaders do not support conditional execution, so gradient reconstruction and illumination are always performed even if no surface is hit, though only once every M steps [S17, unofficial]. Performance: RC-$\beta$ (with empty-space skipping) for Engine dataset (256^2 * 128) achieved 13.6 fps, compared to 6.3 fps for RC and 10.4 fps for SBVR [S17, unofficial]. Choose this for GPU-based isosurface rendering with empty-space skipping [S17, unofficial].
 
 ### Multi-Volume Intersection Highlighting
-A technique to visualize where two or more volumes intersect by shading these regions a particular color (e.g., black) to highlight interactions, determine depth ordering, and identify areas of potential co-channel interference in WiFi data [S17]. For each pixel, it calculates RayDir as the difference between FrontHit and BackHit [S17]. For each sample along RayDir, it initializes hit_volume to false [S17]. For each volume, it samples the texture at the current position (volu, volv, volw) [S17]. If the sample is in the isorange, it checks if hit_volume is already true; if true, it sets colorsample to white (1, 1, 1) and breaks the inner loop; if false, it sets hit_volume to true [S17]. If the sample is not in the isorange or the loop completes without breaking, it shades normally [S17]. This visualization style is best applicable for thin isosurfaces; a different shading model may be necessary for more complicated volumes [S17]. Choose this for visualizing intersections of multiple volumes.
