 ## Background
-The concept of rendering isosurfaces directly from volume data without mesh extraction has roots in early volume visualization research. The Matryoshka algorithm, proposed by Sérgio Lopes, Adriano Lopes, and M. Próspero dos Santos, resembles the work of Udupa and Odhner [UO93] from 1993, where shells of cells of interest were previously stored in lists [S1, unofficial]. The Matryoshka algorithm uses a hybrid approach combining polygon rendering and direct surface rendering, using raytracing for high image quality while avoiding empty ray traversal through optimized interval trees and view-dependent caching [S1, unofficial]. This approach was developed to address the bottleneck of ray-isosurface intersection calculation, acknowledging that traversal steps are more common than intersection calculation steps [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of rendering isosurfaces directly from volume data without mesh extraction has roots in early volume visualization research [S8, unofficial]. The Matryoshka algorithm, proposed by Sérgio Lopes, Adriano Lopes, and M. Próspero dos Santos, resembles the work of Udupa and Odhner [UO93] from 1993, where shells of cells of interest were previously stored in lists [S1, unofficial]. The Matryoshka algorithm uses a hybrid approach combining polygon rendering and direct surface rendering, using raytracing for high image quality while avoiding empty ray traversal through optimized interval trees and view-dependent caching [S1, unofficial]. This approach was developed to address the bottleneck of ray-isosurface intersection calculation, acknowledging that traversal steps are more common than intersection calculation steps [S1, unofficial].
 
